==English==

===Etymology===
Originally from {{bor|en|fr|accostable}}, in later usage partly recoined from {{af|en|accost|-able}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|dated|uncommon}} [[approachable|Approachable]]; [[affable]]; willing to be [[accost]]ed.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1876|title=Our Old Home|author=w:Nathaniel Hawthorne|passage=Old soldiers, I know not why, seem to be more '''accostable''' than old sailors. One is apt to hear a growl beneath the smoothest courtesy of the latter.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Patrick Kasper; et al.|chapter=Modeling User Dynamics in Collaboration Websites|title=Dynamics on and of Complex Networks III: Machine Learning and Statistical Physics Approaches|isbn=9783030146825|page=117|pageurl=https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=9rqXDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA117|text=Online communities becoming increasingly '''accostable''' to their users does not always lead to higher overall activity.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=3 September 2019|author=Jocelyn Silver|title=Renée Zellweger Says Those Mean Tweets About Her Face Made Her Stronger Than Ever|journal={{w|W (magazine)|W}}|titleurl=https://www.wmagazine.com/story/renee-zellweger-plastic-surgery-controversy-bounce-back-mean-tweets|text=“I always felt that I knew what to do in those circumstances,” she continued. “I didn’t feel … '''accostable'''. I never felt that I was being insulted, demeaned. I didn’t recognize it as that.{{nb...}}”}}

